    x86/paravirt: Convert natively patched pv ops to use paravirt alternati=
ves
    =

    With CONFIG_PARAVIRT enabled, the kernel .text is littered with a bunch
    of calls to pv_irq_ops function pointers, like:
    =

      callq  *0xffffffff81e3a400 (pv_irq_ops.save_fl)
    =

    In non-Xen paravirt environments -- including native, KVM, Hyper-V, and
    VMware -- the above code gets patched by native_patch() to look like
    this instead:
    =

       pushfq
       pop    %rax
       nopl   0x0(%rax,%rax,1)
    =

    So in most scenarios, there's a mismatch between vmlinux and the actual
    runtime code.  This mismatch hurts debuggability and makes the assembly
    code harder to understand.
    =

    It also causes the ORC unwinder to produce KASAN warnings like:
    =

      BUG: KASAN: stack-out-of-bounds in deref_stack_reg+0x123/0x140
    =

    This warning is due to the fact that objtool doesn't know about
    parainstructions, so it doesn't know about the "pushfq; pop %rax"
    sequence above.
    =

    Fix both of these issues (debuggability and ORC KASAN warnings) by using
    the alternatives infrastucture to put the native instructions in .text
    by default.  Then, when booting from a hypervisor, replace the native
    instructions with pv ops calls.
    =

    The pv ops calls need to available much earlier than when alternatives
    are normally applied.  So put these alternatives in a dedicated
    ".pv_alternatives" section.
    =

    This has a downside that, in some cases, this makes the paravirt
    patching even more complicated than before.  For example, when running
    on KVM, arch_local_save_flags()code is first patched from native to
    "call *pv_irq_ops.save_fl" by apply_pv_alternatives().  But then, later,
    it's patched back to the native code again, by apply_paravirt().  This
    could probably be simplified by completely redesigning the paravirt
    patching code such that it uses alternatives for all of its patching.
    But that's a bigger project for another day.
    =

    Overall I think this approach will cause less confusion than before
    because the vmlinux code now represents the actual runtime state of the
    code in the most common paravirt cases (native, KVM, Hyper-V, and
    VMware).
    =

    Note this only changes the pv_irq_ops, which are the most commonly used
    ops.  This approach could be easily extended to the other natively
    patched ops in paravirt_patch_*.c.
    =

